+## Operator recovery and low-token background work
+
+Fleet's incident-driven operator surface is self-describing: `fleet --help` and `fleet <command> --help`
+show exact shapes. `result --wait` uses supervisor events with a 10-minute default instead of shell polling;
+`watch` observes one existing supervisor without creating a model turn. The plugin's async Stop hook can wake
+an idle Claude only on meaningful terminal/controller/intervention/stall transitions, so long-running lanes
+do not need fragile `until ... sleep` loops.
+
+`reconcile`, evidence-backed `resolve`, and `archive` provide a supported path out of ambiguous continuation
+reservations and crowded terminal history. `cancel <laneId>` adds a human-friendly preview/confirm shortcut
+while preserving identity binding and returning touched-file evidence. `models --refresh` exposes catalog age
+and refreshes only an idle Fleet-owned app-server. Default status hides archived lanes and warns rather than
+lying when the selected Git worktree ledger is empty but sibling worktree ledgers contain lanes.
+
+Generated implementation/verifier contracts separate start/completion/controller gates and record verification
+as passed/failed/skipped/blocked. They explicitly guard Windows scratch-path/mutation false greens, wrong-worktree
+Python provenance, source-pinning tests, gate blind spots, and visual claims without browser/image evidence.
+See [Harness operations](docs/HARNESS_OPERATIONS.md) and [Troubleshooting](docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md).
+
 ## Project guides
 
